            {
              "criterionId": "compliance",
              "value": 1,
              "rationale": "All requested features present: 30x30 grid (GRID_SIZE=30, 20px cells on a 600px canvas), continuous movement, growth on eating, wall/self collision ending the game, WASD + arrow-key controls, a BFS-pathfinding CPU snake, all three modes (human-human, cpu-human, cpu-cpu), live score board, start menu, game-over screen and R-to-restart."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "works_out_of_box",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "No console errors and controls/animation clearly work (interact-3 shows snakes advanced, interact-2 shows the game-over screen), but gameplay is broken in practice: both snakes spawn on row 15 facing each other 19 cells apart, so with no or normal input the game ends in about one second — interact-2 shows 'GAME OVER / Player 1 hit Player 2!' with 0-0 scores after only ~4.5s of play including two keypresses. There is also no way to restart with R during play (restartGame() is gated on isGameOver), so interact-3's R press does nothing."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "look",
              "value": 1.5,
              "rationale": "Coherent dark theme, readable green/blue player colours, subtle grid, tidy centred menu and overlay panels; plain and unremarkable rather than polished, and the game-over panel covers most of the board, but no visual defects."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code",
              "value": 1
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code_structure",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Reasonable sectioning and naming, but a lot of copy-paste: snake1/snake2 are two parallel globals rather than an array, so spawnFood, the collision checks (five near-identical loops), the reset block and the key handler all duplicate the same logic per snake; moveSnake special-cases `snake === snake1` to pick a DOM element; lightenColor's R/B/G variables are mislabelled (B holds green, G holds blue)."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "logic_correctness",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Core loop, growth, scoring and BFS search are correct, but several real flaws: spawnFood is called before the new head is unshifted, so food can be placed on the cell the head is about to occupy and becomes uneatable until the snake returns; head-swap collisions are missed (heads 1 apart trade cells and pass through each other, only caught incidentally by the body check); collisions are evaluated snake1-first, so identical situations are resolved asymmetrically; the CPU marks its own moving tail as a permanent obstacle and its fallback move has no lookahead, so it walks into dead ends; and the mirrored starting positions make a head-on crash the default outcome."
            }
          ],
