            {
              "criterionId": "compliance",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "All named features exist in code (30x30 grid, growth, wall/self collision, WASD+arrow controls, BFS CPU, three modes, start menu, game over, 'R' restart), but the scoreboard is positioned at top:10px of a 904px-tall container that is vertically centered in a 100vh body with overflow:hidden, so at 1280x800 it is clipped off-screen — the score is not visible in any screenshot during gameplay, so 'display the score during gameplay' is not actually delivered. The 'CPU vs Human' mode also makes snake2 (P2/blue) the CPU while P1 stays human, contradicting its own label ordering."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "works_out_of_box",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "No console errors and the simulation does run and progress, but the game is unplayable: both snakes spawn on the same row (snake1 head x=5 moving right, snake2 head x=14 moving left) 8 cells apart, so at ~120ms/tick they meet and both die after 5 ticks (~0.6s). interact-2 confirms 'GAME OVER / Draw!' after the click plus key presses — every session in every mode ends in a draw within a second unless the player reacts in under half a second. interact-3 ('R' to restart) is visually identical to the game-over state, so the restart keypress produced no observable effect. The board is also clipped top and bottom by overflow:hidden at 1280x800."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "look",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "The menu and game-over screens are tidy and legible (green SNAKE title, styled buttons, red GAME OVER), but the 900px canvas inside a 100vh overflow:hidden body is cut off at both top and bottom, the scoreboard is clipped out of view entirely, and the menu panel's rgba(0,0,0,0.85) card is invisible against the black canvas so it reads as floating text. Playfield rows are hidden from the player."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Readable and commented, but with rough edges: GRID_SIZE (pixel size) vs TILE_COUNT (grid dimension) both equal 30 and are easy to confuse, an unfinished placeholder comment remains in drawSnake ('Simple logic to place eyes based on direction could go here // For now, just center dot'), BFS rebuilds the whole path array with [...path, ...] for every enqueued node, and spawnFood loops over both bodies without early exit."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code_structure",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Sensibly sectioned with clear function names, but snake1/snake2 are two copy-pasted object literals with duplicated init code instead of a factory, the two keydown switch blocks are near-identical duplicates differing only in key names and snake, and the mode dispatch in gameLoop nests a redundant 'cpu-human'/'cpu' check inside an outer test for the same two values."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "logic_correctness",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Core mechanics (movement, growth, wall/self collision, BFS pathfinding with obstacle set and a shuffled survival fallback) read correctly, but the spawn layout is a design bug that makes a head-on mutual kill inevitable in about 0.6s. Head-to-head detection only compares equal cells, so two snakes swapping cells pass through each other undetected (it happens to be caught by the body check, but only by luck). If both heads land on the food in the same tick both score and spawnFood is called twice. The CPU obstacle map treats tails as permanent blockers, making the BFS more conservative than necessary."
            }
