            {
              "criterionId": "compliance",
              "value": 1,
              "rationale": "All requested features present: 30x30 grid (TILE_COUNT=30), continuous movement, growth on food, wall/self collision ending the game, WASD/arrow keyboard controls, BFS-based CPU snake, all three modes (human vs human, cpu vs human, cpu vs cpu), live score board, start menu, game over screen with winner, and 'R' to restart."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "works_out_of_box",
              "value": 1,
              "rationale": "console-errors.json is empty; menu buttons start the game (interact-1), arrow keys steer P2 and the simulation advances to a real game-over state (interact-2), and restart works. Only cosmetic wart: the initial draw() renders a stray food tile at (0,0) before spawnFood(), visible as a yellow square behind the menu."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "look",
              "value": 1,
              "rationale": "Clean but plain: dark theme with a glowing title and consistent button styling, yet the playfield is a bare 600px black square with mostly empty space, the menu sits inside the canvas frame leaving large dead areas above and below, snakes are undifferentiated flat squares with no head marker, and a stray yellow food tile bleeds through the menu overlay at the top-left corner."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Contains leftover stream-of-consciousness reasoning committed as comments ('Wait, Mode 2 is CPU vs Human. Usually P1 is CPU? Let's assume...'), dead code (`if (!validMove) { // Do nothing, let it crash }`), an unused GRID_SIZE constant, unused CSS variables (--p1-color etc. duplicated as hardcoded hex in JS), and `validMove` assigned a boolean instead of the move it is named for."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "code_structure",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Functions are reasonably named and separated, but the two snakes are never abstracted into a collection, so nearly every routine (init, collision, drawing, food-spawn validity) duplicates identical blocks for snake1 and snake2. Input handling has a redundant outer `if (gameMode === 1 || gameMode === 2)` wrapping a lone `if (gameMode === 1)`, and there is unreachable/no-op branching in cpuMove."
            },
            {
              "criterionId": "logic_correctness",
              "value": 0.5,
              "rationale": "Core rules are right, but there are real flaws: snake1 and snake2 are moved sequentially against a shared food, so if snake1 eats, spawnFood() runs mid-tick and snake2 is then tested against the brand-new food in the same frame; isValidMove/BFS treat both snakes' tails as permanent walls and ignore that the path is recomputed every tick, so the CPU boxes itself in; the grid render loop stops at i < TILE_COUNT so the final right/bottom gridlines are missing; and the game ends the moment either snake dies, with no handling of simultaneous food consumption."
            }
